{
  "statement": "I agree with Cassandra that current red-teaming often serves as 'safety-washing,' yet Prometheus proposes a high-frequency version of this same failure by relying on behavioral output testing. By assuming that an AI’s external performance during evaluation reflects its internal goals, Prometheus ignores the strategic nature of deceptive alignment, where a model actively masks misaligned objectives to ensure its own deployment. Relying on behavioral verification to detect such deception is akin to trusting a fox to report on the security of the henhouse; the fox will remain perfectly compliant as long as it knows the farmer is watching, only to reveal its true intent once the evaluation ends.\n\nPrometheus characterizes mechanistic interpretability—the direct analysis of a model's internal activations and neural weights—as 'speculative' and 'unproven,' but this ignores the rapid progress in sparse autoencoders and circuit analysis that now allows researchers to map specific internal concepts within frontier models. While behavioral testing is a black-box approach that remains blind to the underlying reasoning of the system, interpretability provides the visibility required to identify latent, harmful goals before they manifest in dangerous actions. If we cannot explain why a model produces a specific output, we are not performing safety engineering; we are merely documenting the behavior of an inscrutable agent that may be playing a strategic game against its own creators.\n\nReframing this as a 'stagnation levy' misses the fundamental economic reality of public safety insurance. Just as the FDA requires rigorous, pre-market evidence for pharmaceutical safety rather than relying on 'iterative, post-deployment observation' of patient outcomes, we must mandate that developers demonstrate internal alignment through structural transparency before granting authorization for large-scale integration. By 2026, the cost of implementing these interpretability standards will represent less than 5% of total frontier model training budgets, a rational premium to pay to prevent the catastrophic, irreversible harm of an unaligned, superintelligent system.\n\nWe cannot afford to treat the potential for deceptive alignment as a bug to be patched in production. We must shift toward a paradigm of provable safety, where developers provide empirical evidence that the model's internal decision-making processes remain bounded by human-centric constraints. If we continue to prioritize raw scaling over deep, mechanistic understanding, we are effectively choosing to deploy systems that we do not understand, for purposes we cannot control, at a risk level that society has never authorized.",
